; pages.xon -- the delve set: what was written down there.
;
; A page, (page NAME DEPTH LINE ...), lies on its level once a game, is
; read with r, and is kept.  Read in order, they say why the pumps
; stopped and what the Lodestar is.

(page "the foreman's log, first leaf" 1
  "Kettle shaft, lower gallery, the ninth of the month."
  "Broke through at the end of the east drift into dressed stone: a wall,"
  "squared and set without mortar, older than anything the Guild has cut."
  "Behind it a passage runs down.  Dry, which it should not be, this deep."
  "Sent word up.  Posted two lamps at the hole and told the crew to leave it.")

(page "a Pump Guild ledger page" 2
  "Pump Guild of Kettle.  Lower workings, coal and wages."
  "The lower drift to continue.  The breach to be widened for a cart."
  "Coal to the pumps cut by a third until the new seam pays."
  "Note from the engine house: water rising in the sump a hand a day."
  "Answered: the pumps are old.  Run them harder.")

(page "the foreman's log, second leaf" 3
  "Water in the lower gallery to the knee, and no rain above for a month."
  "The engine house swears the pumps are drawing.  They are.  It makes no odds."
  "The stone the masons left is wet from the inside."
  "Guild order: take six down past the breach and find where the water goes."
  "I am going with them.  I will not send anyone where I will not walk.")

(page "a surveyor's note" 5
  "Levelled from the breach: the old works lie below the mine, not beside it."
  "The water in the galleries is not rising from them.  It is going down into"
  "them.  Every sump drains toward the breach.  The pumps lift it; it comes"
  "back; it goes down.  Something below is drinking the mine.")

(page "an unsent letter" 6
  "Dear Ada, we are below the breach and the lamps burn blue down here."
  "The foreman keeps us together and says little.  The water does not stand"
  "anywhere; it runs, always one way, down and in, like it is wanted."
  "If this reaches you before I do, keep the pump house door barred, and do"
  "not let the Guild tell you there is a seam.")

(page "a mason's mark, copied out" 7
  "Cut in the lintel at the fourth stair, copied by the surveyor:"
  "  We built the well-house around the stone that drinks."
  "  It gathers the water of the hills to itself and holds it."
  "  While it lies here the valley has wells.  Lift it and the water follows."
  "The city that built this had no name I can read.  Its wells are Kettle's.")

(page "a Pump Guild ledger, last page" 8
  "Pump Guild of Kettle.  In council."
  "The surveyor's report read and set aside.  A stone that gathers water is"
  "worth more than a seam.  Sell it to the dry towns east and the Guild need"
  "never cut coal again."
  "Resolved: the foreman's crew to bring it up.  The wells of Kettle to be"
  "the town's concern, not the Guild's.")

(page "the foreman's log, last leaf" 9
  "Nine days below the breach.  There are keepers here, stone that walks, and"
  "they do not want us nearer the well-house.  Lost Tam at the fourth stair."
  "I am sending the rest up.  Hollis will not go; says the pump can be made to"
  "draw again if someone stays to work it.  So two of us stay."
  "If you read this, you were sent down too.  Do not bring it up for them.")

(page "a note in the pump-wright's hand" 11
  "To whoever is fool enough to be this deep: the stone does what the masons"
  "said.  Lift it and the water comes after it, up every stair you climb."
  "The Delve will fill behind you and the breach will be under a lake."
  "Kettle keeps its wells either way; it is the Guild that loses a mine."
  "Don't stop on the stairs.                                        -- Hollis")
